('Coordinator','IE',"Welcome to the riverside walking festival. Before you choose a walk, I'd like to explain a few changes to the printed programme and how our activities work. The festival is intended for local people as much as visitors, and none of the walks requires specialist knowledge. We hope you'll notice something unfamiliar even on a street you use every day."),
('Coordinator','IE',"Our first route, the Doorway Walk, looks at how buildings meet the street. You won't go inside private homes. Instead, the guide will ask you to notice entrances, steps, gates and the small spaces where neighbours sometimes stop to talk. It is a short route with frequent pauses, so it suits people who enjoy discussion rather than covering a long distance. It finishes where it begins, at the clock tower."),
('Coordinator','IE',"The Waterline Walk follows the river path. It was advertised as a complete circuit, but repairs to the footbridge mean we must return along the same bank. The total distance is unchanged. This route is mainly about the working river: loading places, old workshop entrances and the ways different activities have occupied the bank. It isn't a wildlife identification walk, though you may certainly see birds."),
('Coordinator','IE',"The third route is called Listening to the Street. For part of it, the guide will ask everyone to stop talking and pay attention to the sounds around them. You don't need to record anything. In fact, we'd prefer phones to remain in pockets during those short periods. Afterwards, there will be time to compare what people noticed. Someone near the front may have heard a different sound from someone at the back, which is part of the interest."),
